    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d5cc6d2_d7d8fb55",
        "filename": "third_party/emdawnwebgpu/library_webgpu.js",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-07T08:16:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe to shield implementation details if we decide to split tables, this should be split into a per-object function as well. Otherwise we wouldn\u0027t know which table to address (or would need a GetType() virtual method, but maybe we don\u0027t want that in emscripten).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "155e1eb383ceab00930770ffeb3c50a6a798cdf0",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3114877_2d769ed4",
        "filename": "third_party/emdawnwebgpu/library_webgpu.js",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-07T21:28:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, that\u0027s a good discussion point... I\u0027ve been thinking about that and I think that we could make this work even if we split the tables.\n\nTo elaborate a bit, I think that keeping the `WGPU*` types as pointers to actual backing structs instead of just an integer will probably be the better route for extensibility, otherwise, it could force us to need to keep two tables, one in JS and one in C as well. That said, if these tables/hashmaps become flat arrays and we want to use simple indices, I think we would bake in the index into a field on the `WGPU*Impl` structs and maybe directly access the heap memory directly via offset with something like `makeGetU32`. I was thinking that we would probably want to use a `uint64_t` to actually hold the indexing information, at which point we could use the top 10ish bits to hold additional table information (since the JS indexing couldn\u0027t go past 53 bits anyways).",
      "parentUuid": "2d5cc6d2_d7d8fb55",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "155e1eb383ceab00930770ffeb3c50a6a798cdf0",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5521d784_582bcf76",
        "filename": "third_party/emdawnwebgpu/library_webgpu.js",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-08T09:03:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It makes sense that we could keep the `WGPU*` actual pointers and store the index in the object. However it\u0027s not immediately clear that we\u0027d want a u64 index, do we expect to go above 4 billion objects of the same type? I agree that the solution as it is right now could be made to work with some magic, but it seems to just be more regular and future proof to have multiple entrypoints and I don\u0027t see any obvious drawbacks. Are we worried about the binary size of the generated library_webgpu.js or are developers going to use `getJsObject` a ton such that typing the type would be annoying?",
      "parentUuid": "b3114877_2d769ed4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "155e1eb383ceab00930770ffeb3c50a6a798cdf0",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fec347e3_3b516670",
        "filename": "third_party/emdawnwebgpu/library_webgpu.js",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-08T19:09:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm that\u0027s true that we probably wouldn\u0027t be able to support that many objects of the same type anyways... but one other concern is that since `getJsObject` is called from JS, there\u0027s no way for us to enforce users to pass the \"right\" typing of `WGPUObject` to it, i.e. users could potentially call `getJsDevice(bufferPtr)` which would result in some wildly invalid code that won\u0027t necessarily fail until later on when the user uses the result. In order to prevent this, we would need to have some typing information inside the object pointer anyways at which point it would probably be easier to only support one entry point to get the objects.\n\nAlso, if we didn\u0027t want to use a u64 for the index, I think using 2 separate pieces of data for for typing and indexing would be fine also, i.e. a u32 for indexing and something like a u8 for typing.\n\nThat said, I\u0027m actually not too opposed to splitting into per-object functions either, aside from the error handling cases. Will wait to see what Kai thinks about this as well.",
      "parentUuid": "5521d784_582bcf76",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "155e1eb383ceab00930770ffeb3c50a6a798cdf0",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3aac8955_01d81bf1",
        "filename": "third_party/emdawnwebgpu/library_webgpu.js",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-09T09:57:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point about the lack of typing information when coming from JS. Maybe we could store the pointer in the WASM heap on the JS object and check its value is the argument pass to `get*`. But that doesn\u0027t work if there are multiple WASM apps using the same object.\n\nWe could also add the typing information to objects only in debug for example if we are worried about the overhead, then `getJsDevice` would `assert` that the type is correct.\n\nLet\u0027s try to find a way forward in the meeting later today. I\u0027d rather not block this CL too long since it is a blocker for the rest of the stack.",
      "parentUuid": "fec347e3_3b516670",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "155e1eb383ceab00930770ffeb3c50a6a798cdf0",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
